From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kexy Biscuit <kexybiscuit@aosc.io>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 13:23:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] AOSCOS: certs: use .balign for certificate list alignment

On MIPS, the GNU assembler interprets ".align 8" as a request for a
256-byte alignment and, worse, moves any label defined immediately
before an alignment directive down to the aligned address ("pulls
down any immediately preceding label to the aligned address", see
s_align() in gas/config/tc-mips.c).

In certs/system_certificates.S, __cert_list_end sits right before an
".align 8", so it is moved past the zero alignment padding (up to 255
bytes) and system_certificate_list_size ends up covering the padding
as well.  At boot, x509_load_certificate_list() walks off the last
certificate into the padding and complains:

    Loaded X.509 cert 'Build time autogenerated kernel key: ...'
    Problem parsing in-kernel X.509 certificate list

This was verified on linux-kernel-7.0.13-2 for loongson3, where
system_certificate_list_size is 1536 while the only embedded
certificate is 1324 bytes long.  The message is harmless but noisy.

Switch to ".balign 8", which is handled by the generic alignment code
with byte semantics and no label moving on every target, making the
resulting layout identical to other architectures (e.g. x86, which
uses the generic handler for ".align" as well).
certs/revocation_certificates.S shares the same pattern and is
converted too.

